  "articleBody": "Putting up basic baseboards in an empty square room is straightforward, but many custom molding projects quickly become tricky. Knowing when to hand the task to a finish carpenter keeps your investment from looking uneven or failing prematurely.\n\nHere are specific signs it is time to bring in professional support:\n\n- Complex stair transitions and angled ceiling pitches. Running crown molding or wainscoting along stairs demands compound miter cuts that require specialized equipment and years of practice.\n- Architectural openings without plumb frames. Older framing frequently bows or twists. A skilled finish carpenter knows how to scribe and shim casings so the final trim looks straight and plumb.\n- Exterior transitions requiring weather protection. Any exterior trim installed around windows, doors, or near masonry requires careful flashing and moisture gaps. For example, keeping appropriate separations from masonry prevents trapped moisture.\n- Matching historic or discontinued woodwork profiles. Sourcing and blending custom casing profiles requires careful measurement and skilled joinery to blend seamlessly.\n\nQuestions to Ask Before You Decide\nAsk your contractor whether their estimate includes prep, hole filling, caulking, and finishing paint. Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC personally oversees residential carpentry, drywall, and finish work in Aurora and Denver communities. Reach out at 303-505-0866 to schedule an on-site consultation.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
